# .audit-ignore.yml — suppress acknowledged audit findings
#
# Place at the repository root. The skill reads this file at Step 4.4
# (between synthesis and money re-validation) and removes matching
# findings from the punch list. Suppressed findings are still listed
# in the report's Appendix D so the team has a paper trail.
#
# Match by fingerprint (the `id` field that begins with `f-`). The
# fingerprint is stable across line moves and whitespace edits but
# unstable across file renames or material code changes — so a fix
# that materially changes the code will produce a new fingerprint
# and the ignore stops applying naturally.
#
# Each entry MUST have:
#   - id:              the finding fingerprint, prefixed `f-`
#   - reason:          non-empty, non-whitespace; explains the acknowledgement
#
# Each entry MAY have:
#   - acknowledged_by: email or handle
#   - expires_at:      ISO date (YYYY-MM-DD); after this date the finding
#                      re-surfaces with a "ignore expired" note
#
# Stale ignores (no current finding matches the id) appear in the report
# under audit.ignore_warnings[] so they can be cleaned up.

- id: f-c1b2c3d4e5f60002
  reason: |
    Bullet gem will be added in the Q3 N+1 audit sprint (LIN-1234).
    Current performance is acceptable per Datadog dashboards.
  acknowledged_by: abkuri88@gmail.com
  expires_at: 2026-09-30

- id: f-c1b2c3d4e5f60003
  reason: |
    The single mismatch (validates_uniqueness_of :referral_code) is
    intentional — codes are tested for collision in Account::RegistrationForm
    before save. Documented in db/migrate/20240101_add_referral_codes.rb.
  acknowledged_by: abkuri88@gmail.com
  # No expires_at — this is a permanent acknowledgement.

# Example of an ignore that has expired — finding will re-surface with
# an "ignore expired YYYY-MM-DD" note in the report.
- id: f-deadbeefdeadbeef
  reason: "Tracking in OPS-456 — was supposed to land by April."
  acknowledged_by: abkuri88@gmail.com
  expires_at: 2026-04-01
